f:T571,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What Hindu scripture should a beginner read first?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Bhagavad Gita is usually the best starting point because it combines philosophy, ethics, devotion, and practice in one text. It speaks directly to the dilemmas of ordinary life."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between Shruti and Smriti?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Shruti refers to the revealed texts, especially the Vedas and Upanishads. Smriti refers to remembered or authored texts such as epics, Puranas, and law or commentary traditions that apply Vedic insight to lived life."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are there so many sacred texts in Sanatan Dharma?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the tradition grew across thousands of years, different regions, teacher lineages, and spiritual needs. Instead of one final book, it preserves a living library suited to different temperaments and stages of practice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need Sanskrit to benefit from these texts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, but learning a few key terms dramatically improves understanding. Even simple words like Dharma, Karma, Atman, and Moksha carry meanings that are richer than their common English approximations."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"sacred-texts-teachings","pillar":"pillar-hub"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"CollectionPage\",\"name\":\"Sacred Texts & Teachings of Sanatan Dharma\",\"description\":\"A pillar hub for the Bhaga