ext-xl font-bold mb-3","children":"Approach the Upanishads after orientation"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"They are dense, luminous, and transformative — but far easier once you know the basic vocabulary of Atman, Brahman, Karma, and Dharma."}]]}],["$","div","Let practice interpret the text",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-white/5 bg-black/10 p-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-orange-400 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-3","children":["Step ",4]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold mb-3","children":"Let practice interpret the text"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Mantra, meditation, and reflection turn scriptural ideas from concepts into insight."}]]}]]}]]}]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"pt-16 border-t border-border","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-center mb-12","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto","children":"Common beginner questions about reading Hindu scriptures and approaching sacred knowledge responsibly."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6","children":[["$","div","What Hindu scripture should a beginner read first?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"What Hindu scripture should a beginner read first?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"The Bhagavad Gita is usually the best starting point because it combines philosophy, ethics, devotion, and practice in one text. It speaks directly to the dilemmas of ordinary life."}]]}],["$","div","What is the difference between Shruti and Smriti?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"What is the difference between Shruti and Smriti?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Shruti refers to the revealed texts, especially the Vedas and Upanishads. Smriti refers to remembered or authored texts such as epics, Puranas, and law or commentary traditions that apply Vedic insight to lived life."}]]}],["$","div","Why are there so many sacred texts in Sanatan Dharma?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"Why are there so many sacred texts in Sanatan Dharma?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Because the tradition grew across thousands of years, different regions, teacher lineages, and spiritual needs. Instead of one final book, it preserves a living library suited to different temperaments and stages of practice."}]]}],["$","div","Do I need Sanskrit to benefit from these texts?",{"className":"rounded-2xl border border-border/60 bg-card p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-lg font-semibold mb-3","children":"Do I need Sanskrit to benefit from these texts?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"No, but learning a few key terms dramatically improves understanding. Even simple words like Dharma, Karma, Atman, and Moksha carry meanings that are richer than their common English approximations."}]]}]]}]]}]
